# yaml-language-server: $schema=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-avc/openavc-drivers/main/avcdriver.schema.json
#
# SMART Board GX Zero, NX and GX (V2) / (V3) interactive displays (RS-232
# room control)
# Reference: SMART's per-model "Managing the display using RS-232" pages on
#            support.smarttech.com (GX Zero, NX, and the GX page with its
#            GX (V2) / GX (V3) series selector), and Appendix C of the
#            installation and maintenance guides: SMART Board GX Zero series
#            (docs.smarttech.com/kb/171956, May 20, 2026) and NX series
#            (kb/171969, March 17, 2026). The GX (V2) and (V3) tables are the
#            same apart from the model numbers; the GX Zero page is the GX (V3)
#            text without the Android input; the NX page spells the USB-C
#            inputs usbc1 / usbc2 and differs on Power Save (below).
# Protocol: ASCII over RS-232, 19200 8N1, no login. A command is
#           "get <setting>" or "set <setting>=<value>" (or +N / -N for a
#           level) ended with CR. Commands are not case-sensitive and extra
#           spacing is ignored. Every reply line is "<setting>=<value>"
#           wrapped in CR LF, followed by the ">" prompt with no line ending,
#           so the prompt reaches the driver as a line of its own in front of
#           the next reply. A word the display does not take is answered
#           "invalid cmd: <the command>".
# Push: the display sends "#<setting>=<value>" (no prompt after it) when its
#       front control panel, Settings app or remote changes power, input,
#       brightness, freeze, screen shade, volume or mute, and when its power
#       state changes. Hybrid: those messages, plus a 15-second poll of the
#       same seven values.
# Power: on / ready / powersave; these models have no Standby. Unlike the RX
#        and MX (V5) pages, none of these pages says any command wakes the
#        display. Each lists the ways out of READY (the Power button, "set
#        powerstate=on", a video signal) and out of POWERSAVE (the Power
#        button, and "set powerstate=ready" or "=on" on the GX Zero and GX;
#        the Power button or a video signal on the NX), and a get is not one
#        of them. So the poll and the start-up reads run in every power state,
#        with no state gate, and the liveness probe below runs on every
#        platform release.
# Families: one RS-232 grammar runs through every SMART Board, but the words
#           differ by family. This driver is the GX / NX dialect (power on /
#           ready / powersave, inputs vga / ops / dp / usbc1 / usbc2, no
#           microphone and no state reporting). The RX and MX are
#           smart_board_rx_mx; the GX (V1) speaks an older grammar at 38400
#           baud (k01gi000) and the GX Plus has no RS-232 page: neither is
#           covered.
#
# Why YAML: plain text get / set lines, one reply line per command, and the
# display's own change messages in the same form on the same line, which
# ConfigurableDriver reads with one rule per setting.
#
# Not verified on hardware (no SMART Board on the bench). Unverified from the
# documents alone: that a get leaves a display in READY or POWERSAVE as it is,
# that the display answers in POWERSAVE (its get powerstate reply lists the
# value, which says it does), which USB-C spelling a GX reports, what an NX
# answers to "set powerstate=on" in POWERSAVE, whether Wake-on-LAN brings an
# NX out of POWERSAVE, and how long the display needs between commands (SMART
# asks only that each reply and prompt arrive before the next command).

id: smart_board_gx_nx
name: SMART Board GX Zero / NX
manufacturer: SMART Technologies
category: display
version: 1.0.0
author: OpenAVC
description: >-
  SMART Board GX Zero, NX and GX (V2) / (V3) interactive displays over RS-232:
  power, input, volume and mute, brightness, freeze and screen shade, with the
  display's own reports of changes made at the display.
source_url: https://support.smarttech.com/docs/hardware/displays/smart-board-gx-zero/en/managing-using-rs-232/default.cshtml
tags: [display, smart, smart-board, interactive-display, interactive-flat-panel, education, rs232]
verified: false
simulated: true
ports: []
min_platform_version: "0.36.0"
compatible_models:
  - manufacturer: SMART Technologies
    models: [SBID-GX065-V3, SBID-GX075-V3, SBID-GX086-V3]
    confidence: untested
    notes: >-
      SMART Board GX Zero (current) and GX (V3) (discontinued), which report the same model number. The GX Zero
      has no built-in operating system, so it refuses the Android input.
  - manufacturer: SMART Technologies
    models: [SBD-NX065, SBD-NX075, SBD-NX086]
    confidence: untested
    notes: >-
      SMART Board NX. SMART's NX page lists only the Power button or a video signal as ways out of Power Save,
      so use Power Off (Ready) for a display a control system turns back on, or Wake (Wake-on-LAN) with the
      display's Wake On LAN setting on.
  - manufacturer: SMART Technologies
    models: [SBID-GX065-V2, SBID-GX075-V2, SBID-GX086-V2]
    confidence: untested
    notes: SMART Board GX (V2) (discontinued). The same commands as the GX (V3).
protocols: [smart_board_rs232]
transport: serial
delimiter: "\r\n"

help:
  overview: >-
    SMART Board GX Zero, NX and GX (V2) / (V3) interactive displays over the
    RS-232 port: power, input, volume, mute, brightness, freeze and screen
    shade. Changes made with the display's buttons, Settings app or remote
    reach OpenAVC as soon as they happen; everything is also read every 15
    seconds, with the screen on or off.
  setup: >-
    1. Connect the display's RS-232 In port with a standard (straight-through)
       RS-232 cable, not a null-modem cable, to a serial port on this computer
       or to a serial bridge (choose "Through a bridge" and pick the bridge's
       port).
    2. The settings are 19200 baud, 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit. Through
       a bridge they are applied to the bridge's port for you.
    3. No login.
    4. NX only, for Wake (Wake-on-LAN): connect the display to the network,
       turn on Wake On LAN in its settings, and type its MAC address (shown in
       its network settings) under MAC Address.
  connection: >-
    The display is read in every power state: Power Off (Ready) and Power Save
    turn the screen off, and OpenAVC reading it does not turn it back on. If
    the display stops answering for about a minute it shows offline, and comes
    back as soon as it answers again.

default_config:
  baudrate: 19200
  bytesize: 8
  parity: "N"
  stopbits: 1
  poll_interval: 15
  inter_command_delay: 0.25
  mac_address: ""

config_schema:
  baudrate:
    type: integer
    default: 19200
    label: Baud Rate
    description: 19200 on the GX Zero, NX and GX (V2) / (V3).
  poll_interval:
    type: integer
    default: 15
    min: 0
    label: Poll Interval (sec)
    description: How often to read the display's settings. 0 turns polling off; changes made at the display still arrive.
  inter_command_delay:
    type: number
    default: 0.25
    min: 0
    label: Inter-Command Delay (sec)
    description: Gap between commands. SMART asks for each reply before the next command is sent; raise this if the display misses commands.
  mac_address:
    type: string
    default: ""
    label: MAC Address (for Wake-on-LAN)
    description: NX only. The display's network MAC address, used by Wake (Wake-on-LAN). Leave it blank if you do not use that command.

state_variables:
  power:
    type: enum
    values: ["on", ready, powersave]
    label: Power
    control: true
    help: "on = picture up. ready = screen off, and Power On, a video signal or the Power button turns it on. powersave = the lowest-power state; the GX Zero and GX come back with Power On, an NX only with its Power button, a video signal or Wake (Wake-on-LAN)."
  input:
    type: enum
    values: [hdmi1, hdmi2, hdmi3, vga, ops, usbc1, usbc2, dp, android]
    label: Input
    control: true
    help: "The active input: hdmi1 to hdmi3, vga, ops (the slot-in PC), usbc1, usbc2, dp (DisplayPort), or android (the display's own screen, NX and GX (V3))."
  volume:
    type: integer
    label: Volume
    min: 0
    max: 100
    step: 1
    control: true
  mute:
    type: boolean
    label: Mute
    control: true
    help: The speakers are muted.
  brightness:
    type: integer
    label: Brightness
    min: 0
    max: 100
    step: 1
  video_freeze:
    type: boolean
    label: Freeze
    control: true
    help: The picture is frozen.
  screen_shade:
    type: boolean
    label: Screen Shade
    control: true
    help: The screen shade is covering the picture.
  model:
    type: string
    label: Model
    help: The model number the display reports. A GX Zero and a GX (V3) report the same one.
  serial_number:
    type: string
    label: Serial Number
  firmware:
    type: string
    label: Firmware
  part_number:
    type: string
    label: Part Number
  last_error:
    type: string
    label: Last Error
    help: "The display's own words for the last command it did not take, such as \"invalid cmd: setinput=android\" on a GX Zero."

commands:
  # Power. Every reply carries the new state, so no read-back get follows.
  power_on:
    label: Power On
    help: Turns the display on from Power Off (Ready), and from Power Save on the GX Zero and GX. An NX in Power Save does not take it; use Wake (Wake-on-LAN) or the display's Power button.
    send: "set powerstate=on\r"
    sets: { power: "on" }
  power_off:
    label: Power Off (Ready)
    help: Turns the screen off and leaves the display ready to come on with Power On, a video signal or its Power button.
    send: "set powerstate=ready\r"
    sets: { power: ready }
  power_save:
    label: Power Save
    help: Puts the display in its lowest-power state. A GX Zero or GX comes back with Power On or Power Off (Ready); an NX only with its Power button, a video signal or Wake (Wake-on-LAN).
    send: "set powerstate=powersave\r"
    sets: { power: powersave }
    confirm: An NX in Power Save does not take Power On. Only its Power button, a video signal, or Wake (Wake-on-LAN) with Wake On LAN turned on in the display brings it back.
  # The NX's Wake On LAN setting (installation and maintenance guide kb/171969,
  # Appendix A); the GX Zero guide has no such setting. RS-232 gives no MAC,
  # so it is typed in. No sets: the guide does not say which power state a
  # wake leaves the display in.
  wake_on_lan:
    label: Wake (Wake-on-LAN)
    help: Sends a Wake-on-LAN packet to the MAC Address under Edit Device (NX only, with the display on the network and Wake On LAN turned on in its settings). Use it to bring an NX out of Power Save.
    available_offline: true
    udp:
      magic_packet: mac_address

  set_input:
    label: Select Input
    help: Selects an input. The GX Zero and GX (V2) refuse Android.
    send: "set input={input}\r"
    sets: { input: "{input}" }
    params:
      input:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Input
        values:
          - { value: hdmi1, label: "HDMI 1" }
          - { value: hdmi2, label: "HDMI 2" }
          - { value: hdmi3, label: "HDMI 3" }
          - { value: vga, label: "VGA" }
          - { value: ops, label: "Slot-in PC (OPS)" }
          - { value: usbc1, label: "USB-C 1" }
          - { value: usbc2, label: "USB-C 2" }
          - { value: dp, label: "DisplayPort" }
          - { value: android, label: "Android (NX and GX (V3))" }

  set_volume:
    label: Set Volume
    help: Sets the speaker volume.
    send: "set volume={level}\r"
    sets: { volume: "{level}" }
    params:
      level:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Volume
        min: 0
        max: 100
  volume_up:
    label: Volume Up
    help: Raises the volume by the step given.
    send: "set volume+{step}\r"
    params:
      step:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Step
        default: 1
        min: 1
        max: 100
  volume_down:
    label: Volume Down
    help: Lowers the volume by the step given.
    send: "set volume-{step}\r"
    params:
      step:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Step
        default: 1
        min: 1
        max: 100
  mute_on:
    label: Mute On
    help: Mutes the speakers.
    send: "set mute=on\r"
    sets: { mute: true }
  mute_off:
    label: Mute Off
    help: Unmutes the speakers.
    send: "set mute=off\r"
    sets: { mute: false }

  set_brightness:
    label: Set Brightness
    help: Sets the screen brightness.
    send: "set brightness={level}\r"
    sets: { brightness: "{level}" }
    params:
      level:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Brightness
        min: 0
        max: 100
  brightness_up:
    label: Brightness Up
    help: Raises the brightness by the step given.
    send: "set brightness+{step}\r"
    params:
      step:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Step
        default: 5
        min: 1
        max: 100
  brightness_down:
    label: Brightness Down
    help: Lowers the brightness by the step given.
    send: "set brightness-{step}\r"
    params:
      step:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Step
        default: 5
        min: 1
        max: 100

  freeze_on:
    label: Freeze On
    help: Freezes the picture on screen.
    send: "set videofreeze=on\r"
    sets: { video_freeze: true }
  freeze_off:
    label: Freeze Off
    help: Unfreezes the picture.
    send: "set videofreeze=off\r"
    sets: { video_freeze: false }
  screen_shade_on:
    label: Screen Shade On
    help: Covers the picture with the screen shade.
    send: "set screenshade=on\r"
    sets: { screen_shade: true }
  screen_shade_off:
    label: Screen Shade Off
    help: Removes the screen shade.
    send: "set screenshade=off\r"
    sets: { screen_shade: false }

device_settings:
  brightness:
    type: integer
    label: Brightness
    help: Screen brightness, 0 to 100.
    state_key: brightness
    default: 100
    setup: false
    min: 0
    max: 100
    write:
      send: "set brightness={value}\r"

quick_actions: [power_on, power_off, mute_on, mute_off]

# Every reply may follow the ">" prompt and a change message starts with
# "#", so each rule allows both in front. First match wins.
responses:
  # The prompt on a line of its own: it carries nothing to read.
  - match: '^>\s*$'
    set: {}
  - match: '(?i:^>?\s*#?\s*powerstate\s*=\s*([a-z]+)\s*$)'
    set: { power: "$1" }
  # The GX pages give each USB-C input as "type-c1 [or usbc1]" and the NX
  # page as usbc1, so either spelling reads as the one the driver sends.
  - match: '(?i:^>?\s*#?\s*input\s*=\s*(?:type-c|usbc)1\s*$)'
    set: { input: usbc1 }
  - match: '(?i:^>?\s*#?\s*input\s*=\s*(?:type-c|usbc)2\s*$)'
    set: { input: usbc2 }
  - match: '(?i:^>?\s*#?\s*input\s*=\s*([a-z0-9]+)\s*$)'
    set: { input: "$1" }
  - match: '(?i:^>?\s*#?\s*volume\s*=\s*(\d+)\s*$)'
    set: { volume: "$1" }
  - match: '(?i:^>?\s*#?\s*mute\s*=\s*(on|off)\s*$)'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: mute, map: { "on": true, "off": false, "ON": true, "OFF": false } }
  # The change message's range is printed as 5 to 100, the set and get as 0
  # to 100; any number is read.
  - match: '(?i:^>?\s*#?\s*brightness\s*=\s*(\d+)\s*$)'
    set: { brightness: "$1" }
  - match: '(?i:^>?\s*#?\s*videofreeze\s*=\s*(on|off)\s*$)'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: video_freeze, map: { "on": true, "off": false, "ON": true, "OFF": false } }
  - match: '(?i:^>?\s*#?\s*screenshade\s*=\s*(on|off)\s*$)'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: screen_shade, map: { "on": true, "off": false, "ON": true, "OFF": false } }
  - match: '(?i:^>?\s*modelnum\s*=\s*(\S.*?)\s*$)'
    set: { model: "$1" }
  - match: '(?i:^>?\s*serialnum\s*=\s*(\S.*?)\s*$)'
    set: { serial_number: "$1" }
  - match: '(?i:^>?\s*fwversion\s*=\s*(\S.*?)\s*$)'
    set: { firmware: "$1" }
  - match: '(?i:^>?\s*partnum\s*=\s*(\S.*?)\s*$)'
    set: { part_number: "$1" }
  - match: '(?i:^>?\s*(invalid cmd\b.*?)\s*$)'
    set: { last_error: "$1" }

# The CR first is SMART's own way to bring up a fresh prompt; it also ends any
# half-typed line left in the display's input. No step waits on the power
# state: a get does not wake these models.
on_connect:
  - "\r"
  - { send: "get modelnum\r", query_for: model }
  - { send: "get serialnum\r", query_for: serial_number }
  - { send: "get fwversion\r", query_for: firmware }
  - { send: "get partnum\r", query_for: part_number }

polling:
  queries:
    - { send: "get powerstate\r", query_for: power }
    - { send: "get input\r", query_for: input }
    - { send: "get volume\r", query_for: volume }
    - { send: "get mute\r", query_for: mute }
    - { send: "get brightness\r", query_for: brightness }
    - { send: "get videofreeze\r", query_for: video_freeze }
    - { send: "get screenshade\r", query_for: screen_shade }

# Over a direct serial line nothing else notices a display that stops
# answering. Any reply counts, an invalid cmd included. Safe in every power
# state for the reason above the poll; the poll has no state gate for it to
# wait on.
liveness:
  send: "get powerstate\r"
  interval: 30
  timeout: 5
  max_failures: 2

# ── Simulator ──
# Models a GX Zero: every reply wrapped in CR LF and followed by the ">"
# prompt, invalid cmd for a word it does not take (Android included), the
# USB-C inputs reported in the GX pages' type-c spelling, and every command
# answered in every power state. Only "set powerstate=on" (and "=ready" from
# POWERSAVE) changes the power state; a get leaves it as it is. Changes made
# in the Simulator UI arrive as "#" messages like the display's own buttons.
# Gaps: a bare CR is not answered (the simulator drops empty lines), a change
# made by a command is announced as well as answered, which the display's
# documents describe only for its own buttons, and a magic packet is logged
# and changes nothing (the GX Zero has no Wake On LAN).
simulator:
  # The display answers inside the gap the driver leaves between commands
  # (SMART asks for each reply before the next command). Left unset, the
  # simulator would answer at the very end of that gap.
  delays:
    command_response: 0.05
  initial_state:
    power: "on"
    input: hdmi1
    volume: 30
    mute: false
    brightness: 80
    video_freeze: false
    screen_shade: false
    model: sbid-gx075-v3
    serial_number: GX75SIM0001
    firmware: "2.1.0"
    part_number: 1031200-A
  controls:
    - type: select
      key: power
      label: Power
      options: ["on", ready, powersave]
    - type: select
      key: input
      label: Input
      options: [hdmi1, hdmi2, hdmi3, vga, ops, usbc1, usbc2, dp]
    - type: slider
      key: volume
      label: Volume
      min: 0
      max: 100
    - type: toggle
      key: mute
      label: Mute
    - type: slider
      key: brightness
      label: Brightness
      min: 0
      max: 100
    - type: toggle
      key: video_freeze
      label: Freeze
    - type: toggle
      key: screen_shade
      label: Screen Shade
  # The display's change messages, the USB-C inputs in the type-c spelling.
  notifications:
    power:
      "*": "\r\n#powerstate={value}"
    input:
      usbc1: "\r\n#input=type-c1"
      usbc2: "\r\n#input=type-c2"
      "*": "\r\n#input={value}"
    volume:
      "*": "\r\n#volume={value}"
    brightness:
      "*": "\r\n#brightness={value}"
    mute:
      "true": "\r\n#mute=on"
      "false": "\r\n#mute=off"
    video_freeze:
      "true": "\r\n#videofreeze=on"
      "false": "\r\n#videofreeze=off"
    screen_shade:
      "true": "\r\n#screenshade=on"
      "false": "\r\n#screenshade=off"
  command_handlers:
    # One dispatcher for the whole grammar, so the power words and the
    # GX Zero's input list are judged in one place.
    - match: '(?i:\s*(get|set)\s*[a-z]+.*)'
      handler: |
        def _run():
            text = match.group(0)
            squeezed = re.sub(r"\s+", "", text).lower()
            m = re.match(r"^(get|set)([a-z]+?)(?:([=+-])(.*))?$", squeezed)

            def say(body):
                respond("\r\n" + body + "\r\n>")

            def word(flag):
                return "on" if flag else "off"

            WIRE = {"usbc1": "type-c1", "usbc2": "type-c2"}
            INPUTS = {"hdmi1": "hdmi1", "hdmi2": "hdmi2", "hdmi3": "hdmi3",
                      "vga": "vga", "ops": "ops", "dp": "dp",
                      "usbc1": "usbc1", "usbc2": "usbc2",
                      "type-c1": "usbc1", "type-c2": "usbc2"}
            BOOLS = {"mute": "mute", "videofreeze": "video_freeze",
                     "screenshade": "screen_shade"}
            LEVELS = {"volume": "volume", "brightness": "brightness"}
            READS = {"modelnum": "model", "serialnum": "serial_number",
                     "fwversion": "firmware", "partnum": "part_number"}

            if not m:
                say("invalid cmd: " + squeezed)
                return
            verb, name, op, value = m.group(1), m.group(2), m.group(3), m.group(4) or ""

            if verb == "get":
                if op:
                    say("invalid cmd: " + squeezed)
                elif name == "powerstate":
                    say("powerstate=" + str(state["power"]))
                elif name == "input":
                    current = str(state["input"])
                    say("input=" + WIRE.get(current, current))
                elif name in LEVELS:
                    say(name + "=" + str(int(state[LEVELS[name]])))
                elif name in BOOLS:
                    say(name + "=" + word(state[BOOLS[name]]))
                elif name in READS:
                    say(name + "=" + str(state[READS[name]]))
                else:
                    say("invalid cmd: " + squeezed)
                return

            # set
            if name == "powerstate" and op == "=" and value in ("on", "ready", "powersave"):
                state["power"] = value
                say("powerstate=" + value)
            elif name == "input" and op == "=" and value in INPUTS:
                state["input"] = INPUTS[value]
                say("input=" + WIRE.get(INPUTS[value], INPUTS[value]))
            elif name in LEVELS and op == "=" and value.isdigit() and int(value) <= 100:
                state[LEVELS[name]] = int(value)
                say(name + "=" + value)
            elif name in LEVELS and op in ("+", "-") and value.isdigit():
                step = int(value) if op == "+" else -int(value)
                level = max(0, min(100, int(state[LEVELS[name]]) + step))
                state[LEVELS[name]] = level
                say(name + "=" + str(level))
            elif name in BOOLS and op == "=" and value in ("on", "off"):
                state[BOOLS[name]] = (value == "on")
                say(name + "=" + value)
            else:
                say("invalid cmd: " + squeezed)
        _run()
    # Anything outside the grammar.
    - match: '.+'
      handler: |
        respond("\r\ninvalid cmd: " + re.sub(r"\s+", "", match.group(0)).lower() + "\r\n>")
